SCREENER 2: include (quote: "hierarchical planning design within multi-agent cooperation to generate exploitable shell responses")

[R0515] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Smart Systems for Electrical, Electronics, Communication and Computer Engineering (ICSSEECC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Improving Generalization of Autonomous SOC Agents through Adversarial Co-Evolutionary Reinforcement Learning
KEYWORDS: Optimization; Training; Modeling; Reinforcement learning; Printing; Learning (artificial intelligence); Security; Convergence; Large language models; Stability; Security Operations Center (SOC); Large Language Models (LLMs); Reinforcement Learning; Adversarial Training; Alternating Optimization; Red Team–Blue Team Simulation; ThreatGraph; Autonomous Cyber Defense; Training Stability; Incident Response Automation
ABSTRACT: Autonomous Security Operations Centers (SOCs) increasingly use Large Language Models (LLMs) and Reinforcement Learning (RL) to automate cyber threat detection and incident response. However, training both Red Team and Blue Team agents at the same time often leads to unstable learning. The attacker and defender continuously change their strategies, which results in inconsistent behavior, slow progress, and policy failure. This study suggests an alternating adversarial training framework for LLM-based network incident response. In this approach, one agent is frozen while the other is optimized. The defender trains against a fixed attacker until it reaches stability. After that, the improved defender is frozen, allowing the attacker to adapt and find new strategies. This alternating process is repeated to ensure stable learning while gradually increasing the challenge of the training environment. The framework is implemented in a ThreatGraph-based enterprise network simulation that reflects realistic incident response processes, such as triage, investigation, remediation, and reporting. The experimental evaluation compares the proposed alternating training strategy with traditional simultaneous adversarial training in the same environments, reward systems, and starting conditions. Performance is measured based on training stability, convergence speed, defender strength against new attack scenarios, and the rate of policy failure. This approach aims to show that alternating adversarial optimization offers a more stable and effective training method for building strong and adaptable autonomous cyber-defense agents.
SCREENER 1: exclude EC1 (quote: "This study suggests an alternating adversarial training framework for LLM-based network incident response.")
SCREENER 2: include (quote: "suggests an alternating adversarial training framework for LLM-based network incident response")

[R0524] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Informatics and Information Technologies (CIIT) | type=CONF
TITLE: Classification and Analysis of Open-Source Gen-AI Penetration Testing Tools
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Tools; Artificial intelligence; Penetration testing; Printing; Machine learning; Computer security; Human in the loop; Security; Servers; cybersecurity; penetration testing; generative ai; model context protocol; retrieval-augmented generation
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is a complex and resource-intensive process that requires deep technical expertise and extensive tooling. Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs), particularly when combined with the Model Context Protocols (MCP), promise to automate penetration testing workflows and lower entry barriers. However, the rapid evolution of this field has created challenges in orientation and assessment. This paper addresses the gap by presenting a systematic methodology for discovering and selecting open-source Gen-AI-based penetration testing tools and classifying them. We conducted a comprehensive GitHub repository search using 14 queries and collected 939 results. By defining and applying rigorous selection criteria, we identified 13 relevant and actively maintained tools. These tools were systematically categorized using a novel categorization framework, covering nine categories: report generation, model support, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) capabilities, state management, interaction models, Penetration Testing Execution Standard (PTES) phase coverage, extensibility, communication interfaces, and deployment models. Our analysis shows that most tools remain in proof-of-concept stages, with MCP wrappers around established penetration testing suites being the most prevalent option. This work provides essential insights for both newcomers and experienced security professionals navigating this emerging field.
SCREENER 1: exclude EC5 (quote: "This paper addresses the gap by presenting a systematic methodology for discovering and selecting open-source Gen-AI-based penetration testing tools")
SCREENER 2: include (quote: "discovering and selecting open-source Gen-AI-based penetration testing tools and classifying them")

[R0532] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE International Conference on Computing (ICOCO) | type=CONF
TITLE: The cyber kill chain for autonomous AI agents
KEYWORDS: Adaptation models; Feedback loop; Generative AI; Computer architecture; Planning; Control theory; Artificial intelligence; Computer security; Optimization; Next generation networking; adaptive; AI agents; autonomous; cognitive; cybersecurity; cyber kill chain
ABSTRACT: This paper introduces the Autonomous Attack Chain (AAC), a framework designed to conceptualise and model the distinct phases of offensive cyber behaviour executed by artificial intelligence (AI) agents. Optimised for human-led attacks, traditional cyber kill chain frameworks often inadequately capture the dynamic, adaptive, and cognitive capabilities inherent in modern AI systems. The AAC addresses this gap by synthesising foundational theories, including related cognitive architecture models such as Belief-Desire-Intention, Observe–Orient–Decide–Act, Goals-Plans-Action, and the Perceptual Control Theory. Conceptualised as a continuous, cyclical process centred around an AI agent’s Cognitive Core (a generative AI model), the AAC outlines phases such as Planning Initiation, Data/Tool Acquisition, Execution and Adaptation, Persistence/Propagation, and Impact Optimisation. This model explicitly accounts for the agent’s ability to reason over uncertain environments, form internal representations of success, and dynamically adjust actions based on environmental feedback, embodying the adaptive and learning affordances crucial to autonomous cyber operations. Thus, the AAC provides a better understanding of AI-driven threats, laying essential groundwork for developing advanced, adaptive defensive strategies.
SCREENER 1: exclude EC3 (quote: "The AAC addresses this gap by synthesising foundational theories, including related cognitive architecture models")
SCREENER 2: include (quote: "distinct phases of offensive cyber behaviour executed by artificial intelligence (AI) agents")

